{-
   How many clusters the text has, with an ASCII fast path a register at a time.

   **In an all-ASCII run every unit is a cluster except that CR LF is one**, since no ASCII character
   joins anything but LF to a CR (`clusterEnd`'s fast path says why). So a block of ASCII starting at
   a boundary holds its width in clusters less its CR LF pairs, which is two compares, an `and` and a
   `popcnt`. The pairs are found by comparing the block for CR and the block one byte later for LF,
   so the byte after the block has to be ASCII too.

   **A pair across two blocks needs no care.** It is taken off in the block holding its CR, and the
   next block starts at its LF and counts that LF as a cluster, so the two blocks together count the
   pair once. That block may not start at a boundary, but nothing it does needs one: another ASCII
   block counts units, and a walk from an LF ends right after it, since GB4 breaks after every LF.

   **A block with anything else in it backs off for four registers**, walked cluster by cluster
   before the next test. Retrying after one register cost CJK text 8% at v2 and Latin and emoji text
   5% at v3 against the plain walk: text that is not all ASCII rarely has a whole register of it,
   so the test fails nearly every time. With the back-off every corpus in the benchmark is within 2%
   of the plain walk, and all-ASCII text still counts 32 times as fast at v2 and 70 times at v3. The
   walk's end is capped at the text's, since `clusterEnd` stops there.
-}
pub fn countClusters(text: String) -> Size:
    let bytes = String.bytes(text)
    let size = length(bytes)
    let width = vectorBytes
    let zero = zero() :: Vec(I8)
    let returns = splat(13 :: I8) :: Vec(I8)
    let feeds = splat(10 :: I8) :: Vec(I8)
    let &clusters = 0 :: Size
    let &at = 0 :: Size

    while at + width < size:
        let block = bitcast(loadVector(bytes, at)) :: Vec(I8)

        if none(block .< zero) && bytes[at + width] < 0x80:
            let next = bitcast(loadVector(bytes, at + 1)) :: Vec(I8)
            clusters += width - (count(and(block .== returns, next .== feeds)) :: Size)
            at += width
        else:
            let stop = min(at + 4 * width, size)
            while at < stop:
                at = clusterEnd(text, at)
                clusters += 1

    while at < size:
        at = clusterEnd(text, at)
        clusters += 1

    return clusters
